WEBVTT

00:00:02.090 --> 00:00:03.950
We are Team Vibecoders.

00:00:03.950 --> 00:00:08.238
Welcome to TidePlan: repayment, in rhythm with life.

00:00:08.238 --> 00:00:13.092
This is our working microloan cash-flow planning prototype.

00:00:13.092 --> 00:00:23.609
It asks a practical question: not just whether someone can repay, but when they can repay without sacrificing essential expenses.

00:00:23.609 --> 00:00:29.354
The demonstration uses synthetic records and does not move real money.

00:00:30.555 --> 00:00:33.618
We begin with Asha, a smallholder farmer.

00:00:33.618 --> 00:00:38.849
Twenty-four months of history reveal changing income across the year.

00:00:38.849 --> 00:00:42.885
Her remaining obligation is eighteen thousand rupees.

00:00:42.885 --> 00:00:49.162
With a two-thousand-rupee monthly buffer, equal payments cause two buffer breaches.

00:00:49.162 --> 00:00:56.784
Aligning payments with available capacity schedules the same total with no breaches in this scenario.

00:00:56.784 --> 00:01:00.147
The chart makes the timing mismatch visible.

00:01:01.350 --> 00:01:07.881
The evidence brief explains the recommendation using the same calculations as the chart.

00:01:07.881 --> 00:01:16.714
It compares recent earnings with the previous year, identifies lean months, and shows the assumptions behind capacity.

00:01:16.714 --> 00:01:22.727
The historical chart separates income from essential expenses and existing debt.

00:01:22.727 --> 00:01:30.150
These are transparent rules, not an unexplained credit score or an AI-generated financial decision.

00:01:31.350 --> 00:01:37.597
Meera, a shop owner, has a different pattern of earnings and repayment capacity.

00:01:37.597 --> 00:01:45.251
Ravi, a delivery partner, shows a sustained decline compared with the same months a year earlier.

00:01:45.251 --> 00:01:50.952
TidePlan flags this change for a conversation and exposes the shortfall.

00:01:50.952 --> 00:01:59.622
It does not explain every downturn away as seasonality or pretend that changing dates can solve every problem.

00:02:00.822 --> 00:02:02.494
Now open Plan studio.

00:02:02.494 --> 00:02:08.548
The protected buffer is money left after essential costs and existing debt.

00:02:08.548 --> 00:02:12.849
Raising it reduces the room available for repayments.

00:02:12.849 --> 00:02:17.707
The income-shock control asks what happens if earnings fall.

00:02:17.707 --> 00:02:23.203
A fifteen-percent conservative haircut is applied before scheduling.

00:02:23.203 --> 00:02:29.814
These are scenario assumptions, not guaranteed income or calibrated probabilities.

00:02:29.814 --> 00:02:33.558
Every change updates the chart and comparison.

00:02:34.762 --> 00:02:40.878
At a sixty-percent income shock, the proposed schedule cannot cover the obligation.

00:02:40.878 --> 00:02:47.511
The app shows the unscheduled debt, the buffer shortfall, and a disabled approval button.

00:02:47.511 --> 00:02:55.323
Approval is also blocked when living costs breach the buffer even if the debt can be scheduled elsewhere.

00:02:55.323 --> 00:03:02.914
This is where a human review, additional support, or separately assessed loan restructuring is needed.

00:03:04.118 --> 00:03:10.844
Returning to a five-percent shock gives us a manageable scenario for this demonstration.

00:03:10.844 --> 00:03:18.335
The comparison shows total scheduled debt, months below the buffer, and any remaining obligation.

00:03:18.335 --> 00:03:27.660
Below it, each month lists conservative income, safe capacity, fixed payments, aligned payments, and cash left afterward.

00:03:27.660 --> 00:03:33.469
The monthly schedule can be downloaded as a CSV for review outside the app.

00:03:34.673 --> 00:03:37.983
The prototype also accepts a monthly CSV.

00:03:37.983 --> 00:03:45.494
Download the sample to see the required columns: month, income, essentials, and obligations.

00:03:45.494 --> 00:03:49.935
Records must cover twelve to sixty consecutive months.

00:03:49.935 --> 00:03:55.992
Invalid files are rejected instead of silently producing a recommendation.

00:03:55.992 --> 00:03:58.899
Here we import the complete sample.

00:03:58.899 --> 00:04:05.441
Data is processed in this browser, and the selected loan amount stays unchanged.

00:04:06.648 --> 00:04:10.539
Next, the demo lender approves the exact scenario.

00:04:10.539 --> 00:04:18.320
The borrower sees the total and monthly schedule, explicitly checks their acceptance, and confirms.

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:25.013
A saved approval is bound to the underlying data, safety buffer, shock, and payments.

00:04:25.013 --> 00:04:34.196
These role buttons demonstrate the workflow; production authentication and independent identities remain future work.

00:04:34.196 --> 00:04:37.775
No financial commitment is made by this demo.

00:04:38.981 --> 00:04:44.206
Changing the safety buffer after acceptance makes this a different plan.

00:04:44.206 --> 00:04:49.650
The app no longer treats the old approval as approval of the new scenario.

00:04:49.650 --> 00:04:55.529
Return to the original assumptions and the accepted version is recognised again.

00:04:55.529 --> 00:05:03.005
This keeps an experimental what-if analysis separate from the schedule the borrower actually reviewed.

00:05:04.207 --> 00:05:07.787
The trust ledger brings the agreement together.

00:05:07.787 --> 00:05:11.901
Lender approval and borrower acceptance are recorded.

00:05:11.901 --> 00:05:22.107
A current, consented shock scenario can receive a simulated one-thousand-rupee sponsor grant, reducing the displayed reserve balance.

00:05:22.107 --> 00:05:25.535
The grant is capped by available demo funds.

00:05:25.535 --> 00:05:30.638
It neither transfers money nor silently changes the loan schedule.

00:05:30.638 --> 00:05:34.903
The reserve is support, not a lock on borrower savings.

00:05:36.107 --> 00:05:42.741
Every recorded event includes a cryptographic fingerprint and a link to the previous event.

00:05:42.741 --> 00:05:50.541
Verify the chain, inspect the recorded evidence, and export the audit history or complete decision report.

00:05:50.541 --> 00:05:58.123
We can test a deliberately modified copy: verification rejects it while the original remains unchanged.

00:05:58.123 --> 00:06:05.195
A local hash chain detects edits, but cannot stop the browser owner replacing the whole history.

00:06:06.400 --> 00:06:10.523
The methodology page explains the complete pipeline.

00:06:10.523 --> 00:06:15.201
React and TypeScript power the interface and calculations.

00:06:15.201 --> 00:06:20.831
Recharts visualises the comparisons, and Papa Parse handles CSV input.

00:06:20.831 --> 00:06:23.606
GitHub Pages hosts the static app.

00:06:23.606 --> 00:06:33.359
Hyperledger Fabric and a sponsor reserve smart contract are separate integration blueprints, not connected infrastructure.

00:06:33.359 --> 00:06:39.544
No live language model or bank connection is required for this demonstration.

00:06:40.755 --> 00:06:46.299
TidePlan is designed for a focused pilot with lenders and borrower groups.

00:06:46.299 --> 00:06:56.564
We would measure affordability, forecast error, borrower costs, and recovery together, rather than claim success from a dashboard alone.

00:06:56.564 --> 00:07:06.379
The project supports the intent of Sustainable Development Goal eight: more inclusive financial services and stronger livelihoods.

00:07:06.379 --> 00:07:08.178
We are Team Vibecoders.

00:07:08.178 --> 00:07:14.547
Understand the cash flow, compare the options, agree clearly, and keep the evidence.

